<% content_for(:title, "About · Cyvasse") %>
<%# Ported from the original site's about page (legacy home/about.html.erb).
    Every external link opens a new tab with rel=noopener. Links whose
    original target was a personal domain are dropped: those domains can lapse
    and be re-registered (cyvasse.io now serves spam). %>

<article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto about-page">
  <%= render "pages/banner", image: "backgrounds/cyvasse_about_background.png", title: "About" %>

  <section id="author" class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center sm:items-start gap-6 mb-10">
    <figure class="shrink-0 text-center">
      <%= link_to "https://www.linkedin.com/in/amcritchie", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" do %>
        <%= image_tag "thanks/alexmcritchie.jpg", alt: "Alex McRitchie", class: "about-portrait rounded-full object-cover" %>
      <% end %>
      <figcaption class="mt-2 font-semibold text-heading">Alex McRitchie</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="rules-prose space-y-4">
      <p>
        <%= link_to "Cyvasse", "https://awoiaf.westeros.org/index.php/Cyvasse", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %>
        is a fictional game from
        <%= link_to "George R. R. Martin's", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_R._R._Martin", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %>
        series <i><%= link_to "A Song of Ice and Fire", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Song_of_Ice_and_Fire", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %></i>,
        played in
        <%= link_to "Essos", "https://awoiaf.westeros.org/index.php/Essos", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %>
        and
        <%= link_to "Dorne", "https://awoiaf.westeros.org/index.php/Dorne", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %>.
        The true rules of Cyvasse are not known, save by George R. R. Martin
        himself, and it may be the case that he has never developed a complete
        set of rules. The goal of this website is to allow fans to get together
        and play each other using a set of rules I developed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="story" class="rules-prose space-y-4 mb-12">
    <p>
      Though the rules of Cyvasse are not specified in great detail in any of
      the books, we are given hints on how the game is played. In preparation
      for creating the rules, I diligently went over the entire
      <i>A Song of Ice and Fire</i> series multiple times, including
      <i><%= link_to "The World of Ice and Fire", "https://awoiaf.westeros.org/index.php/The_World_of_Ice_and_Fire", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %></i>
      and
      <i><%= link_to "The Hedge Knight", "https://awoiaf.westeros.org/index.php/The_Hedge_Knight", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %></i>,
      paying special attention to
      <i><%= link_to "A Feast for Crows", "https://awoiaf.westeros.org/index.php/A_Feast_for_Crows", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %></i>
      and
      <i><%= link_to "A Dance with Dragons", "https://awoiaf.westeros.org/index.php/A_Dance_with_Dragons", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer" %></i>,
      where Cyvasse is referenced. The rules used in this imagining combine the
      specifics found in the books with my views on game design.
    </p>
    <p>
      This project started on August 8, 2014, as a personal project for gSchool,
      a full-stack web development boot camp. gSchool played a crucial role in
      the development of Cyvasse, as this is where I learned the skills I used
      to build this site.
    </p>
    <p>
      If nothing else, this site is a love letter to my favorite author,
      George R. R. Martin, as well as an attempt to solidify a set of rules that
      reflects the specifics and spirit of Cyvasse we learn about throughout
      <i>A Song of Ice and Fire</i>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy" class="card p-5 mb-12">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-heading mb-2">Your messages</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary leading-relaxed">
      Messages you send another player are shown to the two of you, and site
      admins can read them too, to keep the game friendly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="thanks" class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading text-center mb-2">Thanks</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary text-center leading-relaxed max-w-xl mx-auto mb-6">
      I want to give a special thanks to the people who have been my friends and
      mentors through this whole journey.
    </p>

    <ul class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <% [
           [ "Jeff Taggart", "thanks/jefftaggart.jpeg", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/jetaggart" ],
           [ "Bobby Wilson", "thanks/bobbywilson.jpg", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/bobbywilson0" ],
           [ "Bobby Blackstock", "thanks/bobbyblackstock.jpeg", nil ],
           [ "Sean Smith", "thanks/seansmith.jpeg", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/seansmith1020" ],
           [ "Zach Klabunde", "thanks/zachklabunde.jpeg", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/zbunde" ],
           [ "Aaron Gray", "thanks/aarongray.jpg", nil ]
         ].each do |name, photo, href| %>
        <li class="thanks-profile card p-4 text-center">
          <% portrait = image_tag(photo, alt: name, loading: "lazy", decoding: "async", class: "thanks-portrait rounded-full object-cover mx-auto mb-2") %>
          <%= href ? link_to(portrait, href, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener noreferrer") : portrait %>
          <span class="font-semibold text-heading text-sm"><%= name %></span>
        </li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="gschool" class="card p-5 text-center mb-6">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-heading mb-2">gSchool g[3]</h3>
    <p class="text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mx-auto mb-4">
      Last and most importantly, I want to thank gSchool for being the greatest
      group I have ever had the privilege of being a part of. I love you all!
    </p>
    <%= image_tag "thanks/gschool.jpg", alt: "The gSchool g[3] class", loading: "lazy", decoding: "async", class: "w-full rounded-lg" %>
  </section>
</article>
